Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:02.550 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  in his famous novel the plague albert  camus writes quote we learn in time of  pestilence that there are more things to  admire in men than to despise you could  say that about the coronavirus and the  aids epidemic that started 40 years ago  on this world aids day it's estimated  about 36 million people have died from  hiv-related causes and about 38 million  people are living with the virus and  there is still no cure our chief medical  correspondent dr john lapook reflects on  his own experience working at the height  of the aids crisis he went back to a  place central to that time in his life  in the fall of 1976 i entered medical  school i was eager to make a difference  but when you're a first year medical  student you don't know a whole lot of  medicine  so my contribution to clinical care was  basically limited to bringing my college  singing group to the hospital to  entertain sick children  i graduated in may 1980 and off i went  to start my internship as a newly minted  doctor at what was then called columbia  presbyterian medical center  then in march of 1981 federal health  officials consider it an epidemic yet  you rarely hear a thing about it i saw  my first patient with what would become  known as aids he was suffering from  something i had never seen before  an eye infection from a common virus  that a normal immune system could  usually handle but his immune system was  anything but normal  [Music]  he died  and for years so did every single  patient with aids who i helped care for  so many of them were in what should have  been the prime of their lives  they all had so-called opportunistic  infections that attacked people with  weakened immune systems hiv the virus  that causes aids  would not be discovered for another two  years  but we were all discovering first hand  that it could decimate the immune system  we're all scared to death we'd like to  know what the hell we're supposed to do  here what should we do should we stay  home and lock ourselves back in the  closet or you know what should we do we  don't know what we're dealing with and  it's deadly and it's transmissible  wow and it was right here in this very  amphitheater  where students and staff got together  for what was called grand rounds trying  to figure out what in the world was  going on  we all felt helpless  and i certainly didn't think i was  making a difference  i'm 31 years old  and i've watched  a great many of my friends die sometimes  patients would get better for a while if  their infections were treatable but they  would always get readmitted to the  hospital i remember one man especially  well a car mechanic in washington  heights  a husband and a father  a lovely guy who could not have been  more grateful for the care we gave him  even though our care didn't prevent his  death  we want to live  that is all we want america then in 1987  for the first time a drug to treat aids  patients got final government approval  today the drug azt is not a vaccine  against the disease nor a cure it was  astonishing  finally some hope  and thanks to scientific research the  treatment gradually improved over the  years and hiv aids became a more chronic  though often still devastating condition  [Music]  over the next 30 years images of those  brutal early days remained with me  but they mostly stayed in the background  then ten years ago i saw the normal  heart  a gut-wrenching play about the early  days of the epidemic  at the end the names of people who had  died of aids were projected on the walls  of the theater  i couldn't move my eyes filled with  tears  as the sadness of that time came  flooding back  those memories were a big reason why in  april of 2020 i helped out on the covet  wards at nyu langone health where i'm a  professor of medicine i wanted to share  what i had learned with the young  students doctors and nurses who might be  feeling as helpless as i did in 1981.  they were way ahead of me they had  already arranged for their patients who  were in isolation to video chat with  loved ones  or kept them company by sitting at the  bedside they understood the therapeutic  value  of human touch  of reaching out and holding a hand  they also seem to understand it was okay  to shed a tear or two and it was okay to  take advantage of mental health services  over the years i've learned there are  all sorts of ways to make a difference  and it's almost never something as  dramatic as saving somebody's life  much more often it's by making their  journey gentler  by showing them compassion and empathy  by listening  by being present  bravo john lepouk i'm telling you you  know he is  to me one of the most empathetic caring  people that i've that i've ever met so  it's no surprise to see early in his  career that he was still that same  person yeah his message is so important  and so true especially on this world  aids day which was essentially created  to unite everybody around the world to  try and fight this deadly disease and to  bring empathy to it my mom's brother  uncle ricky passed away from aids  in 1990 i was nine years old  um  that forced us to become familiar with  aids yes and that familiarity turned  into education yep so that piece was  remember in the beginning we were so  afraid nobody knew what we were dealing  with oh thank you john it was tough back  then because no one knew what it was no  one knew what it was

